  "query_preview": "..A low-level jet continues early this morning through \nthe Gulf of Mexico and northern Sonora, pumping moisture into the \ndeserts of western and southern Arizona. Shallow instability and \nupslope have enabled light to moderate showers to continue, with \nisolated to scattered coverage. This pattern will continue through \nthe day today, as 700mb flow and the upslope component continues to \nstrengthen. The moisture profiles are quite robust, initially the \nthinking was that showers activity would largely pause today, but \nnow it looks like we will be a bit more active. The upslope regions \nsouth of the Mogollon Rim will continue to see scattered coverage \nthroughout the day.\n\nBy late this afternoon and into the evening, the aforementioned \ncomponents ramp up even further, along with the arrival of a deeply \nsaturated sub-tropical plume and associated jet. This will quickly \nincrease the coverage and strength of rainfall, with large areas of \nthe state seeing nearly continuous moderate to heavy rain heading \ninto Sunday. Even as the plume begins to get shunted southward, the \narrival of a strong cold front from the north will further enhance \nrainfall Sunday evening and overnight. The FROPA will also feature a \nlocalized severe threat as it moves southeast Sunday night.\n\nMesoscale models now have peaks within the upslope regions ranging \nfrom 4 to 6+ inches of rain, with at least 1 to 3, locally 4 inches \nin most other areas. This will cause major and serious hydrological \nissues, especially since we are already saturated in many locations. \nA second FFA is in effect for the entire CWA during this time frame.\n\nBehind the final trough passage Monday morning, we quickly dry out \nwith strong ridging developing for a few days. Long-range ensembles \nare hinting at yet another cut-off low forming near the end of next \nweek, potentially bringing more showers and thunderstorms.\n\nThis is a dangerous situation, especially pertaining to flooding. \nBoth flash flooding and then longer term river flooding will be a \nproblem. Larger stem waterways may be flooding for days after the \nevent, especially the Little Colorado River and the Verde River.\n\nAgain, we cannot stress enough, THIS IS A SERIOUS SITUATION. Stay \ntuned to your favorite weather source and find a way to receive \nwarnings. STAY AWAY FROM WATERWAYS, TURN AROUND, DON'T DROWN!",
